             <p>Prove that \(A\) and \(A^{\top}\) have the same nonzero singular values.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>Let \(\lambda > 0\) and let \(E\) be the eigenspace of \(A^{\top}A\) for \(\lambda\). The map \(\mathcal{L}(v) = Av\) sends \(E\) into the eigenspace of \(AA^{\top}\) for \(\lambda\), because \(AA^{\top}(Av) = A(A^{\top}Av) = \lambda Av\). It is injective on \(E\), because \(\|Av\|^2 = v^{\top}A^{\top}Av = \lambda\|v\|^2\) for \(v \in E\), so \(Av = 0\) forces \(v = 0\). Hence \(\dim E \le \dim E'\), where \(E'\) is the eigenspace of \(AA^{\top}\) for \(\lambda\). The same argument with \(A\) and \(A^{\top}\) exchanged gives \(\dim E' \le \dim E\). So the two eigenspaces have equal dimension for every \(\lambda > 0\). Both matrices are symmetric, so the positive eigenvalues of \(A^{\top}A\) and \(AA^{\top}\) agree with multiplicity, and by Theorem 2.3 the nonzero singular values of \(A\) and \(A^{\top}\) agree, under Assumption F1 of Lesson 4 (Theorem 2.3).</p>
